<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Glomma</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Glomma is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, about 620 kilometres long. It rises near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roros">Røros</a> and flows south through the Østerdalen valley and the farmlands of Romerike and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostfold">Østfold</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skagerrak">Skagerrak</a> at Fredrikstad. For centuries it carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> floated down from the inland forests to sawmills on the coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/glomma.jpg" alt="Glomma" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo: Jack R. Johanson · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lakelandscape800.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
